{"id": "sevt_01GAGykaLgKZweJGooU7QSeS", "content": [{"text": "**Checkout p99 regression: commit 371dfe2 replaced a batched `order_items` query with one query per open order.**\n\n**Deploy timeline (last 6h)**\n- 13:02 `inventory` e0a27f7: unrelated to checkout.\n- 14:06:20 `checkout` 371dfe2 (alice): \"refactor order summary builder for readability.\" Deployed successfully.\n- 14:18:10 `checkout` db46df5 (carol): \"temporarily disable catalog cache.\" Rolled back 14:23:40.\n\n**Gap against metrics**\n- p99 begins climbing at 14:06, one minute after 371dfe2 deployed. It goes from about 80 ms to about 400 ms by 14:28.\n- It steps to 2152 ms at 14:29 and reaches about 3.7 s at 14:30. There is no deploy at 14:29.\n- db46df5 landed at 14:18, after the climb had already started. The rollback at 14:23 did not reverse the trend; p99 kept rising to about 5 s. So db46df5 is not the driver.\n\n**Diff for 371dfe2**\n- The old code ran one `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)` and grouped the results in memory.\n- The new code loops over open orders and issues `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.order_id)` for each one. This is the classic per-row (N+1) query pattern, which the runbook lists as the most common cause.\n\n**Log confirmation (app.log, checkout service)**\n- Before 14:06, `order_items` queries are batched (`IN (?)`), about 1 to 6 per minute.\n- From 14:06 on, per-row queries (`order_id = ?`) appear, about 20 to 50 per minute, and then 60 to 140 per minute by 14:30 to 14:40.\n- Per-query durations are short individually, but the request count multiplies. Per-minute average duration rises from about 5 ms to about 13 ms.\n- `db_pool_utilization` for checkout climbs steadily from about 0.15 at 14:00 to 1.0 at 14:38 and stays there. Pool saturation follows the query fan-out, so it is a consequence rather than the trigger.\n- GC pauses (G1 young gen, 88 to 223 ms) start at 14:29:57, the same minute as the p99 step. They are likely an amplifier that adds to the queueing delay.\n\n**Open questions**\n- I have not pinned down why the p99 step lands at 14:29 rather than tracking the gradual ramp. The likely explanation is that the per-row fan-out crosses a concurrency or pool threshold around then, together with the GC pauses. I did not check request volume or per-host breakdowns to confirm this.\n- I did not check whether the `auth` identity-provider timeouts at 14:15 affect checkout. They look separate.\n\n**Recommended next steps**\n- Revert 371dfe2, or restore the batched query, and watch p99 and `db_pool_utilization` recover.\n- Add a test or query-count assertion to catch N+1 regressions in this handler.\n- Carol's cache change is already rolled back. Re-enable `@cached` on `load_catalog_page` once INC-2241 is understood.\n\n**Root cause:** `371dfe2` \u2014 replacing the single batched `order_items` query with a per-order query multiplies database round-trips per checkout request, which saturates the DB connection pool and drives p99 from about 80 ms to over 5 s.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T12:01:20.603439Z", "type": "agent.message"}
